    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d204">

                <pb facs="0325" n="249" xml:id="pg249"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.512351 Double/247</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Straus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1769</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-04-09">April 9, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        17.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to confirm my telegram No.
                    310 of April 9, 11 a.m., 1935,<anchor n="49" xml:id="d204-f1"/> stating that the
                    exchange of ratifications of the double taxation convention and protocol of
                    April 27, 1932, took place this morning at the Quai d’Orsay. There is sent under
                    separate cover, for the Department’s archives, the French instrument of
                    ratification as well as a copy of the protocol attesting to the exchange signed
                    by the Minister for Foreign Affairs, Monsieur Laval, and myself.</p>
                <p>As a matter of record I also transmit herewith an excerpt from the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Journal Officiel</hi> of April 6, 1935,<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d204-f2"/> containing the text of the law by which the French
                    Parliament approved the convention, signed by the President of the Republic on
                    April 5, 1935.</p>
                <p>It should be reported that at the time of the exchange of ratifications no
                    mention was made by the French authorities of the resolution of Parliament
                    providing for approaching the American Government on the subject of securing for
                    French interests further assurances with regard to taxation in the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jesse Isidor
                    Straus</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
